[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is a bounded task — one existing static page, one small addition — so I'll present a short design here rather
  than write a spec.

  [1mContext:[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is a single static page with an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m. No CSS, no JS, no build step, no tests.

  [1mDesign[0m

  The page has no task items yet, so "mark items as done" needs something to attach to. Smallest thing that satisfies
  the request:

  - Add a task item inside [38;5;153m<main>[39m: a [38;5;153m<label>[39m wrapping [38;5;153m<input type="checkbox">[39m plus the item text. Native checkbox, so
    on/off state is the browser's — no JS for the toggle itself.
  - Add a tiny inline [38;5;153m<style>[39m so a checked item renders visibly done (strikethrough via [38;5;153minput:checked + span[39m).
  - No persistence (no localStorage), no add/delete, no framework. State resets on reload.

  [1mFiles touched:[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m only.

  [1mTesting:[0m none — no test infrastructure here, and this is static markup. I'd verify by opening the page and toggling.

  Two assumptions worth flagging, since you can correct them now rather than after:
  1. One hardcoded sample item is enough (vs. a list of several, or a reusable JS component that renders items from
     data).
  2. State does not need to survive a reload.

  Does this look right?

